WEBVTT

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:05.560
in the name of the Father and of the Son and of the Holy Spirit. Amen!

00:00:05.560 --> 00:00:12.560
The reading from the Sinaxadion for October 10th, on which we commemorate,

00:00:12.560 --> 00:00:16.560
our venerable and God-bearing Father Ambrose of Optina.

00:00:16.560 --> 00:00:23.700
Our Holy Father Ambrose, the most outstanding figure among the Staretsi,

00:00:23.700 --> 00:00:30.700
elders of the monastery of Optina, was born in the year 1812.

00:00:31.700 --> 00:00:37.700
He was a lively child of quick understanding, and his grandfather, a village priest,

00:00:37.700 --> 00:00:44.700
took charge of his early education, bringing him up in the study of church books.

00:00:44.700 --> 00:00:50.700
When he went to the seminary for his secondary education, as was then customary,

00:00:50.700 --> 00:00:58.700
he did brilliantly as a student, but without the intention of becoming a priest or monk.

00:00:59.700 --> 00:01:05.700
Towards the end of his studies, he fell seriously ill and made a vow to enter a monastery

00:01:05.700 --> 00:01:08.700
if he recovered.

00:01:08.700 --> 00:01:17.700
Once he was better, he put off fulfilling his promise, worked for a while as a tutor in a landed family,

00:01:17.700 --> 00:01:24.700
and then spent some time teaching Greek at the junior seminary in Loupetsk.

00:01:24.700 --> 00:01:29.700
His jovial, sociable nature was the cause of the fooling around,

00:01:29.700 --> 00:01:36.700
as he called it later, that kept him in the world, but his conscience gave him no rest,

00:01:36.700 --> 00:01:41.700
and he spent many hours in prayer and tears.

00:01:41.700 --> 00:01:46.700
God called him and reminded him of his promise by all sorts of signs.

00:01:46.700 --> 00:01:51.700
So, as he was leaning over the bank of a stream one day,

00:01:51.700 --> 00:01:55.700
he heard a voice in the flowing water that murmured,

00:01:55.700 --> 00:02:01.700
praise God, retain God.

00:02:01.700 --> 00:02:05.700
After four years of indecision, he could bear it no longer,

00:02:05.700 --> 00:02:12.700
and went to ask the advice of a holy hermit called Ilarion, who lived in the district.

00:02:12.700 --> 00:02:16.700
The statets received him with a smile and said,

00:02:16.700 --> 00:02:21.700
Go to Optina, you are needed there.

00:02:21.700 --> 00:02:27.700
The young man was obedient to the will of God and went without delay to the hermitage of Optina

00:02:27.700 --> 00:02:34.700
in the diocese of Caluga near the town of Kozelsk, a monastery that was on its way

00:02:34.700 --> 00:02:42.700
to becoming the center of a widespread movement of spiritual renewal in Russia.

00:02:42.700 --> 00:02:46.700
He was first put to serve statets Leonid,

00:02:46.700 --> 00:02:51.700
who because of his old age entrusted him to the direction of statets Makarios,

00:02:51.700 --> 00:02:55.700
who had taken over his spiritual responsibilities

00:02:55.700 --> 00:03:00.700
and was working on an extensive addition of patristic texts.

00:03:00.700 --> 00:03:07.700
The young monk received the tonture in the year 1842, with the name of Ambrose.

00:03:07.700 --> 00:03:12.700
Three years later, he was ordained priest.

00:03:12.700 --> 00:03:20.700
As he was proficient in the ancient languages, Ambrose became one of Makarios' closest fellow workers

00:03:20.700 --> 00:03:24.700
in the preparations of his editions of the Holy Fathers,

00:03:24.700 --> 00:03:30.700
especially of the Russian translation of the latter of St. John Klimakos.

00:03:30.700 --> 00:03:36.700
His familiarity with patristic texts and their application to everyday life

00:03:36.700 --> 00:03:39.700
through frequent discussions with his spiritual father,

00:03:39.700 --> 00:03:45.700
with the best possible preparation for succeeding him as statets.

00:03:45.700 --> 00:03:52.700
But here again, Providence stepped in and altered drastically the course of his life.

00:03:52.700 --> 00:03:57.700
A serious illness brought him within an inch of death,

00:03:57.700 --> 00:04:02.700
and for the remainder of his days he was more or less bedridden,

00:04:02.700 --> 00:04:10.700
unable to serve the divine liturgy and relieved of all monastic obedience.

00:04:10.700 --> 00:04:15.700
This was God's way of reigning in his genial temperament,

00:04:15.700 --> 00:04:20.700
thus giving him the opportunity of greater self-knowledge

00:04:20.700 --> 00:04:26.700
through retiring within himself and discovering within the depths of his heart,

00:04:26.700 --> 00:04:33.700
the mysteries of human nature and the means of reconciling men with God.

00:04:33.700 --> 00:04:37.700
Illness gave him experiential knowledge of the truth

00:04:37.700 --> 00:04:42.700
that it is in our weakness that the power of God is revealed.

00:04:42.700 --> 00:04:49.700
As he would later affirm, it is good for a monk to have passed through illness.

00:04:49.700 --> 00:04:54.700
When he is sick, he ought not to strive to be cured completely,

00:04:54.700 --> 00:04:56.700
but only by half.

00:04:56.700 --> 00:05:04.210
Statets Makarios began sending brethren of the monastery to him for spiritual counsel,

00:05:04.210 --> 00:05:08.210
and the bishop gave him the blessing to assist the statets,

00:05:08.210 --> 00:05:13.210
in the task of confessing the ever-growing crowd of faithful from all walks of life,

00:05:13.210 --> 00:05:17.210
who flocked to Optina in order to quench their thirst

00:05:17.210 --> 00:05:22.210
at the pure springs of evangelic wisdom.

00:05:22.210 --> 00:05:27.210
St. Ambrose welcomed all patiently, judged the faults of none,

00:05:27.210 --> 00:05:32.210
and gave his advice simply as a disciple of the Holy Fathers,

00:05:32.210 --> 00:05:36.210
without ever expressing a personal opinion.

00:05:36.210 --> 00:05:41.210
The action of grace and unceasing inner prayer gave him an extraordinary insight

00:05:41.210 --> 00:05:44.210
into the hidden depths of consciences,

00:05:44.210 --> 00:05:50.210
so that a slight illusion or a few words was all he needed to show his visitors

00:05:50.210 --> 00:05:54.210
the solution to their problems.

00:05:54.210 --> 00:05:59.210
He never used this gift of insight to judge or blame,

00:05:59.210 --> 00:06:03.210
but waited on care-worn souls with a sympathy and tenderness

00:06:03.210 --> 00:06:07.210
that were truly maternal, so that they should understand that

00:06:07.210 --> 00:06:13.210
the goodness of God leads us to repentance.

00:06:13.210 --> 00:06:17.210
On the death of St. Ambrose in the year 1860,

00:06:17.210 --> 00:06:22.210
Ambrose was called to exercise a ministry that soon extended to the whole of Russia.

00:06:22.210 --> 00:06:28.210
Rich and poor, learned and unlettered, nobles and simple folk,

00:06:28.210 --> 00:06:31.210
noted members of the intelligentsia,

00:06:31.210 --> 00:06:34.210
they all made their way to this better-written man

00:06:34.210 --> 00:06:40.210
as to a prophet newly risen among them, seeking a word of counsel

00:06:40.210 --> 00:06:43.210
or of consolation in their distress,

00:06:43.210 --> 00:06:48.210
a word of salvation that was to order their entire life,

00:06:48.210 --> 00:06:53.210
a call to repentance, or a simple blessing.

00:06:53.210 --> 00:06:57.210
Despite his physical weakness and his ailments,

00:06:57.210 --> 00:07:01.210
Ambrose received them all cheerfully and would adapt his manner

00:07:01.210 --> 00:07:04.210
and his words to each person,

00:07:04.210 --> 00:07:07.210
so that after conversing with the statets of optina,

00:07:07.210 --> 00:07:12.210
the life of many of his visitors changed completely.

00:07:12.210 --> 00:07:16.210
People who spoke with him noticed how he could pass immediately

00:07:16.210 --> 00:07:23.210
from the discussion of high theology to the solution of practical problems of everyday life.

00:07:23.210 --> 00:07:27.210
Someone criticized him one day for wasting his time

00:07:27.210 --> 00:07:32.210
advising a peasant woman how to feed her turkeys.

00:07:33.210 --> 00:07:36.210
Don't you understand, he answered,

00:07:36.210 --> 00:07:40.210
that her whole life is bound up with these turkeys

00:07:40.210 --> 00:07:44.210
and that the peace of her soul is of no less account

00:07:44.210 --> 00:07:50.210
than that of those who come to me with questions about sublime matters?

00:07:50.210 --> 00:07:56.210
The writer Tolstoy visited the statets and was greatly edified.

00:07:56.210 --> 00:08:00.210
I did no more than converse with him, he wrote,

00:08:00.210 --> 00:08:05.210
and somehow my soul lost all heaviness.

00:08:05.210 --> 00:08:08.210
When you talk with such a man,

00:08:08.210 --> 00:08:13.210
you feel the nearness of God.

00:08:13.210 --> 00:08:18.210
Another writer speaks of an unfathomable abyss of charity

00:08:18.210 --> 00:08:24.210
within the saint whose love was extended to all.

00:08:24.210 --> 00:08:29.210
The statets lived in the skit at a short distance from the monastery.

00:08:29.210 --> 00:08:33.210
His day began about 4 a.m.

00:08:33.210 --> 00:08:36.210
After reading Mattins with his cell servants,

00:08:36.210 --> 00:08:39.210
he spent three hours in prayer.

00:08:39.210 --> 00:08:43.210
Then from 9 a.m. he began to receive the visitors,

00:08:43.210 --> 00:08:47.210
first the monks, and then the laypeople,

00:08:47.210 --> 00:08:50.210
who were waiting in a long queue outside.

00:08:50.210 --> 00:08:55.210
These meetings went on until 11 p.m.

00:08:55.210 --> 00:08:59.210
with a short pause for a frugal meal.

00:08:59.210 --> 00:09:04.210
During his few spare moments, he would deal with his vast correspondence,

00:09:04.210 --> 00:09:08.210
dictating more than forty replies a day to letters

00:09:08.210 --> 00:09:12.210
that he often had no need to open.

00:09:12.210 --> 00:09:17.210
For about thirty years the blessed statets persevered in this ascetic labor

00:09:17.210 --> 00:09:22.210
that was harder than many anaskecies performed in the peace of the desert.

00:09:22.210 --> 00:09:26.210
A number of monks helped him in his task,

00:09:26.210 --> 00:09:29.210
particularly his cell servant Joseph,

00:09:29.210 --> 00:09:34.210
who succeeded him as statets of optina.

00:09:34.210 --> 00:09:38.210
Rather than great ascetic efforts which can be infected with self-love

00:09:38.210 --> 00:09:42.210
when they are not checked by an exact obedience,

00:09:42.210 --> 00:09:45.210
the statets commended repentance to his visitors

00:09:45.210 --> 00:09:52.210
and the humble contrition of heart that is acquired by turning one's life towards God

00:09:52.210 --> 00:10:00.210
and by hating sin with all one's strength for the origin of all sin is pride.

00:10:00.210 --> 00:10:04.210
Besides his ability in the cure of souls,

00:10:04.210 --> 00:10:09.210
St. Ambrose organized associations of devout persons in many places

00:10:09.210 --> 00:10:14.210
to undertake charitable work and to assist orphans and the poor.

00:10:15.210 --> 00:10:18.210
Ever since the destructive reform of Catherine II,

00:10:18.210 --> 00:10:24.210
it had been impossible for the daughters of poor families to lead the monastic life.

00:10:24.210 --> 00:10:31.210
For each monastery had to demand a large dowry of every young woman who applied to enter.

00:10:31.210 --> 00:10:34.210
During the last ten years of his life,

00:10:34.210 --> 00:10:39.210
statets Ambrose sought to remedy this unjust state of affairs

00:10:39.210 --> 00:10:47.210
by founding and organizing a convent for nuns at Shamordino about nine miles from optina.

00:10:47.210 --> 00:10:51.210
Through his paternal care, this monastery was,

00:10:51.210 --> 00:10:58.210
in a short time, inhabited by more than 1,000 nuns from every rank in society

00:10:58.210 --> 00:11:01.210
who strove to live in perfect charity,

00:11:01.210 --> 00:11:07.210
a life based on the traditions of the Holy Fathers around the person of statets Ambrose,

00:11:07.210 --> 00:11:12.210
their spiritual father, and living image of Christ.

00:11:12.210 --> 00:11:17.210
In addition to the conventional buildings, the statets built an asylum,

00:11:17.210 --> 00:11:22.210
a school, a hospital, and a hospice for aged women,

00:11:22.210 --> 00:11:26.210
so that the place became truly a city of Christian love

00:11:26.210 --> 00:11:31.210
that attracted crowds of destitute, sick and unfortunate folk

00:11:31.210 --> 00:11:36.210
for whom the monastery was a haven of hope and consolation.

00:11:37.210 --> 00:11:43.210
The needs of the convent obliged the statets to be at Shamordino often,

00:11:43.210 --> 00:11:47.210
and when they began building a stone church,

00:11:47.210 --> 00:11:52.210
he found he would have to stay there to oversee the work of construction.

00:11:52.210 --> 00:11:56.210
His absence led to rumors among the monks at optina

00:11:56.210 --> 00:11:59.210
and disturbed the bishop of Caluga.

00:11:59.210 --> 00:12:05.210
The saint spent the whole summer of 1891 at Shamordino,

00:12:05.210 --> 00:12:09.210
attending to all the business matters that concerned the convent,

00:12:09.210 --> 00:12:15.210
which the new abyss who lacked administrative ability could not deal with.

00:12:15.210 --> 00:12:19.210
It appeared that Ambrose was in a hurry to finish his task

00:12:19.210 --> 00:12:23.210
and was preparing for his departure hence.

00:12:23.210 --> 00:12:29.210
At the beginning of autumn, overwhelmed with fatigue, he finally fell ill.

00:12:29.210 --> 00:12:33.210
When he was told that the bishop had decided to come in person

00:12:33.210 --> 00:12:36.210
to the convent to send him back to optina,

00:12:36.210 --> 00:12:40.210
he received the bad news with complete composure.

00:12:40.210 --> 00:12:46.210
And on the 10th of October, in the year 1891,

00:12:46.210 --> 00:12:52.210
the day before the episcopal visit, he gave up his soul to God.

00:12:52.210 --> 00:12:56.210
Until his last hour, he continued to receive the faithful

00:12:56.210 --> 00:12:58.210
who crowded around his cell.

00:12:58.210 --> 00:13:02.210
So it was that he fulfilled the prophetic words that he said again

00:13:02.210 --> 00:13:05.210
and again to his disciples.

00:13:05.210 --> 00:13:09.210
I have spent my whole life in the midst of the people,

00:13:09.210 --> 00:13:13.210
and that is how I shall die.

00:13:13.210 --> 00:13:16.210
All Russia wept with his passing.

00:13:16.210 --> 00:13:19.210
He was laid to rest at optina,

00:13:19.210 --> 00:13:24.210
beside Staretz Leonid and Staretz Macarius.

00:13:24.210 --> 00:13:28.210
On his tombstone are carved the words,

00:13:29.210 --> 00:13:32.210
to the weak I became weak,

00:13:32.210 --> 00:13:35.210
that I might win the weak.

00:13:35.210 --> 00:13:38.210
I have become all things to all men,

00:13:38.210 --> 00:13:43.210
that I might by all means, save some.

00:13:43.210 --> 00:13:46.210
Through the prayers of our holy fathers, O Lord,

00:13:46.210 --> 00:13:51.210
Jesus Christ our God, have mercy on us and save us.
